    </div>

</div>


    
    
    <div class="containers tn-accordion parbase">
    <div class="accordion" id="accordion-23364e225e834840b5b3e8351e1f34c8">
        

        
            

            
                <div class="accordion-item">
                    

                    
                        <div class="accordion-heading">
                            <span class="accordion-title">
                                <button class="accordion-button icon-angle-right collapsed" type="button" data-bs-toggle="collapse" data-bs-target="#collapse-23364e225e834840b5b3e8351e1f34c8-0" aria-controls="collapse-23364e225e834840b5b3e8351e1f34c8-0" aria-expanded="false">
                                    City of Memphis Solid Waste Earth Complex Tier Two Compost Facility
                                </button>
                            </span>
                        </div>

                        <div id="collapse-23364e225e834840b5b3e8351e1f34c8-0" class="accordion-collapse collapse" data-bs-parent="#accordion-23364e225e834840b5b3e8351e1f34c8">
                            <div class="accordion-body panel-body" tabindex="0">
                                <div>




    
    
    <div class="tn-rte">
    
        <p>The Tennessee Department of Environment and Conservation (TDEC), Division of Land Protection (DLP) - Waste Management Branch (WMB), has issued a permit major modification to the City of Memphis. The Tier II compost facility, identified as Earth Complex Composting and Recycling Center (CMP790000011), is located in Shelby County at 2389 Hennington Avenue, Memphis, Tennessee (latitude 35.064473 N/longitude -90.14416 W).</p>
<p>This permit would allow for expansion and continuance of construction, operation, and closure of the Tier II composting facility for waste materials classified as ground yard waste from commercial landscapers, the general public, and public works organizations, as well as pre-consumer and post-consumer food waste originating from residential, commercial, and industrial sources, including, but not limited to, homes, restaurants, produce packing, animal processing, and grocery stores, and/or certain wastes having similar characteristics and approved in writing by TDEC. This major modification specifically allows compost operations to extend onto two adjacent parcels adding the potential for 9.2 acres of additional operational area. No hazardous wastes, as regulated by the Tennessee Hazardous Waste Management Act (Tennessee Code Annotated, Section 68-212-101, et. seq.) and the Rules adopted pursuant to the Act, shall be accepted for disposal at this facility. The official responsible for operation of the facility is Mr. Philip Davis, City of Memphis Solid Waste Director, 125 North Main Street, Room 628, Memphis, TN 38103 (901) 576-6872.</p>
<p>The WMB made the draft of this permit available for review during a 45-day public comment period which ended on June 1, 2026. The WMB received no oral or written comments during the above-mentioned public comment period.</p>
<p>The final permit and response to comments are posted to the final page of the Permit Documents section of Dataviewer linked below. Two locations also have paper copies available for review:</p>

    
    

</div>


    
    
